-- title:
--   Pole divisor degree at most [F:K(x)]
-- statement:
--   Let $K$ and $F$ be fields with $F$ a $K$-algebra satisfying `IsCurveOver K F`: every nonzero $f \in F$ has a finitely supported divisor recording its orders, $D(v) = \operatorname{ord}_v f$ at every place $v$, of degree $0$; every place has residue field finite over $K$; and $\Omega[F/K]$ is free of rank one over $F$. Here a place $v$ of $F/K$ is a valuation subring of $F$ containing $\operatorname{im}(K \to F)$, distinct from $F$ itself, which is a principal ideal ring, $\operatorname{ord}_v$ is the associated normalised additive valuation, a divisor is a finitely supported $\mathbb{Z}$-valued function on places, and $\deg D = \sum_v D(v)\deg v$. Assume in addition that the Riemann–Roch space of the zero divisor, $\{f \in F : v(f) \le 1 \text{ for all } v\}$, is finite-dimensional over $K$. Let $x \in F$ be transcendental over $K$ and assume $F$ is finite-dimensional over the intermediate field $K(x)$ generated by $x$. Let $D$ be a divisor with $D(v) = \max(0, -\operatorname{ord}_v x)$ for every place $v$, i.e. the pole divisor of $x$. Then $\deg D \le [F : K(x)]$, the inequality taken in $\mathbb{Z}$.
--
--   This is one half of the classical identity $\deg (x)_\infty = [F:K(x)]$ for a one-variable function field, the direction given by the fundamental inequality $\sum_{P \mid \infty} e_P f_P \le [F:K(x)]$ for the extensions of the infinite place of $K(x)$, and it requires no separability assumption. It feeds into [`AlgebraicCurve.Divisor.degree_eq_finrank_adjoin_of_eq_max_neg_ord`](thm.html#AlgebraicCurve.Divisor.degree_eq_finrank_adjoin_of_eq_max_neg_ord), where the two inequalities are combined into the equality.
-- source:
